/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1031 "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Zero or Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1033 3278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1034 0.643339719890591 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050770 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6550b696-b7fd-5957-83f4-7ee02f7386ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161564 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "53075006-3bde-5335-a07e-7b2f11fef0a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "574876ef-03aa-5a40-bf88-67cee41508d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161835 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161835 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fd51d8d8bc91a4702ad01324e7e444f099276dfd80e77e260fa9944d0dac9f6c.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $2\\times 2$ matrix that  could be the matrix $V.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16e5cc04-aa74-55f7-992b-b9992a8dd1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161835 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=6$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-36[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 36I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/d7/d7fa1eea4f89ef21e80d7e090d4487447cfc7a78b6edc99df4b3fd4fe51ab03a.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/2c/2cc8e66369af5363ecfe712442910d939308452a61405e77c311bb3bc993bbae.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/61/6149abf5eabb91a50f0bee958e2a1fbaf547b75f10437f5449d8e06ea0789153.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161836 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161836 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b9/b9a8dca1b62edcadcef99761c807fb080c90b361e13d1a1d85a3b76ee11c2327.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bba077a6-56c6-5137-b949-26fae1c98625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161836 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=\\sqrt5$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ \nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have a third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/e8/e86a2c3e4c978a2dc3e5baf3b5ff756cf8274e4927b48728372525d0f4167dab.svg)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} x_1 + \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} x_2 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1 + 2x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/05/057b025491639af10c2d86155680847f3adbd60b200bf01a31c86c7105dfb761.svg)\n\nwhere $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. Notice that the two linearly independent solutions are mutually orthogonal. Normalizing these vectors, we get \n\n![](images/53/53dafbde7f215cade420c760ceed34dd14bdfe8ef5b707df7970d7e2ca4dc8a7.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/42/42df8be6e8f84ac199e75726881ecd1b2e459470f40e24e3fd676ec7970ca31b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161837 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161837 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/224eae90a2f7d511bfacc312b65e03550d2a16193dcbe37744cbf22832c95a7a.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a vector whose components correspond to the $3$rd column of the matrix $U.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4fd6d412-d080-5fd1-9318-65a0f5f02dc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161837 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of an $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then \n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=7$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/59/59f2aaa5e80215ac83784b4856cc50950e055d905df0275751bfd74f8b402b12.svg)\nsuch that \n![](images/13/13bf6231db3b846372b7c204aed3ceb8d817ff5b89926b488a57c7f6fc45e0e1.svg)\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n![](images/37/372ed41f2b087a67c84a6bfab37eca038996f82ff39b34e1c4060bac513dca2d.svg)\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n![](images/ce/ced06b533ddd43db1f1d81640b33cd64408a71250bf72b52873fae7d407a6654.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3e/3e5668a36411c0f93c4eb5f3debd0f79341f17447f6ac94c574724477a1f9deb.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161839 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161839 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5c23820c65f2ff70de6eb9f10e6e99f6daaf8096e0cb873fb2c3d68946e2f4c1.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find a $3\\times 3$ matrix that could be the matrix $U.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b9d8b05-cdb6-555b-b68c-b46000bb6650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161839 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/a1/a1256c5e8c454d905c1550c06bf3aa413cc17acaeba0e6e2cbbda68cfc05ac50.svg)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{2} x_2- \\dfrac{1}{2} x_3 = 0   \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\sqrt2x_1 + x_2-x_3 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n![](images/a6/a619cda4912587e8c4dc4da0bc7d0a575d1c10f1adba2137099f347f6d7eabb3.svg)\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n![](images/22/22311430e9529d48b9d0460011e6baceacfbfcc54902439e917bfdc3dd04d5db.svg)\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing the orthogonal vectors $\\mathbf{c}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U{:}$   \n\n![](images/66/661e2d509c842bac2a5427e82c3973feb972b540dbf405be7a023a7802cf93fb.svg)\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1a27507e18b6bcb0353668919f7fb5c356afa1f07ab77f73422fe3236e78fe8a.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162041 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388813 #edb/ref 