86371415 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371416 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7f3af859-ec67-5d0a-9aa6-e199fc99b8e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371417 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32682b95-8a4f-5b69-b420-e85ce95344d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371418 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1316725-d805-544f-8c76-97e1ffa13f7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371419 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371419 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371419 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d92f9873-426c-51b2-986d-aa34df746743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371420 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371420 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371420 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c761380-456b-5c6c-ad7b-855413aa94fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371421 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371421 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371421 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dfda4152-a1cf-5e55-9f89-6fc134d8adc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd52405b-2747-5b7b-9ac8-728557a18405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371422 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #e